  "worked_solution": "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{1-2x} & \\le 3\\cdot {5}^{2-3x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061({4}^{1-2x}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3\\cdot {5}^{2-3x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061({4}^{1-2x}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3)+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({5}^{2-3x}) \\\\ (1-2x)\\operatorname{ln}\u20614 & \\le \\operatorname{ln}\u20613+(2-3x)\\operatorname{ln}\u20615 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u20614-2x\\operatorname{ln}\u20614 & \\le \\operatorname{ln}\u20613+2\\operatorname{ln}\u20615-3x\\operatorname{ln}\u20615 \\\\ 3x\\operatorname{ln}\u20615-2x\\operatorname{ln}\u20614 & \\le \\operatorname{ln}\u20613+2\\operatorname{ln}\u20615-\\operatorname{ln}\u20614 \\\\ x(3\\operatorname{ln}\u20615-2\\operatorname{ln}\u20614) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}\u20613+2\\operatorname{ln}\u20615-\\operatorname{ln}\u20614 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u20613+2\\operatorname{ln}\u20615-\\operatorname{ln}\u20614}{3\\operatorname{ln}\u20615-2\\operatorname{ln}\u20614}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
